Here the graph is stored in a matrix instead of an adjacency list. The matrix also prevents the same prerequisite pair from increasing the count twice. This uses more memory, but it gives a direct way to check every possible dependent course.

For example, with `N = 3` and pairs `[1, 0]`, `[2, 1]`, the queue starts with course `0`. Removing `0` unlocks `1`, and removing `1` then unlocks `2`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool canFinish(int N, vector<vector<int>> arr) {
        vector<vector<bool>> edge(N, vector<bool>(N, false));
        vector<int> indegree(N, 0);

        for (const vector<int>& pair : arr) {
            int course = pair[0];
            int prerequisite = pair[1];

            if (!edge[prerequisite][course]) {
                edge[prerequisite][course] = true;
                indegree[course]++;
            }
        }

        queue<int> queue;
        for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
            if (indegree[course] == 0) {
                queue.push(course);
            }
        }

        int completed = 0;
        while (!queue.empty()) {
            int finished = queue.front();
            queue.pop();
            completed++;

            for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
                if (edge[finished][course]) {
                    indegree[course]--;
                    if (indegree[course] == 0) {
                        queue.push(course);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return completed == N;
    }
};
```

The matrix is the main trade-off. It is useful for avoiding duplicate edges, but it can be large when `N` is large. A self-dependency never reaches the queue and correctly returns `false`.

Time complexity: `O(N^2 + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(N^2)`. The input array is left unchanged.

Instead of building a graph, this Java version keeps the prerequisite pairs as they are. Whenever a course is completed, it scans the whole pair list and removes that course from every matching dependency. It is less efficient than an adjacency list, but the steps are easy to follow.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canFinish(int N, int[][] arr) {
        int[] remaining = new int[N];

        for (int[] edge : arr) {
            remaining[edge[0]]++;
        }

        java.util.ArrayDeque<Integer> queue = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
            if (remaining[course] == 0) {
                queue.add(course);
            }
        }

        int completed = 0;
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int finished = queue.remove();
            completed++;

            for (int[] edge : arr) {
                int course = edge[0];
                int prerequisite = edge[1];

                if (prerequisite == finished) {
                    remaining[course]--;
                    if (remaining[course] == 0) {
                        queue.add(course);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return completed == N;
    }
}
```

This handles an empty prerequisite list because all courses start in the queue. A cycle leaves its courses with a positive count, so they are never completed.

Time complexity: `O(N + N * E)` due to scanning all pairs after each completed course.
Space complexity: `O(N)`. The input array is not changed.

The prerequisite list can also be checked by following each course toward its prerequisites. `active` contains the courses on the current recursive path. If a course appears there again, the path has formed a cycle. `done` avoids checking safe courses more than once.

Dry run for `arr = [[1, 0], [0, 1]]`: checking course `0` visits `0`, then `1`, then reaches `0` again in `active`, so the result is `False`.

```python
class Solution:
    def canFinish(self, N, arr):
        prerequisites = [[] for _ in range(N)]
        for course, prerequisite in arr:
            prerequisites[course].append(prerequisite)

        active = set()
        done = set()

        def dfs(course):
            if course in active:
                return False
            if course in done:
                return True

            active.add(course)
            for prerequisite in prerequisites[course]:
                if not dfs(prerequisite):
                    return False

            active.remove(course)
            done.add(course)
            return True

        for course in range(N):
            if not dfs(course):
                return False

        return True
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)` because each course and prerequisite edge is processed at most once.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)` for the lists, sets, and recursion stack. This code does not modify `arr`.

A prerequisite pair `[course, pre]` means there is an edge from `pre` to `course`. I count how many prerequisites each course still needs, then start with courses whose count is zero. If all courses leave the queue, there is no cycle.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool canFinish(int N, vector<vector<int>> arr) {
        vector<vector<int>> graph(N);
        vector<int> indegree(N, 0);

        for (const vector<int>& edge : arr) {
            int course = edge[0];
            int prerequisite = edge[1];
            graph[prerequisite].push_back(course);
            indegree[course]++;
        }

        queue<int> ready;
        for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
            if (indegree[course] == 0) {
                ready.push(course);
            }
        }

        int completed = 0;
        while (!ready.empty()) {
            int course = ready.front();
            ready.pop();
            completed++;

            for (int next : graph[course]) {
                indegree[next]--;
                if (indegree[next] == 0) {
                    ready.push(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return completed == N;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`, where `E` is the number of prerequisite pairs.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`. The input array is not changed.

This version checks for a cycle with DFS. State `1` means the course is currently being explored, while state `2` means it was fully checked. Reaching a course with state `1` means the current path came back to itself.

The graph stores `prerequisite -> course`, so after finishing a prerequisite, DFS checks the courses it can unlock.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canFinish(int N, int[][] arr) {
        java.util.List<java.util.List<Integer>> graph = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            graph.add(new java.util.ArrayList<>());
        }

        for (int[] edge : arr) {
            int course = edge[0];
            int prerequisite = edge[1];
            graph.get(prerequisite).add(course);
        }

        int[] state = new int[N];
        for (int course = 0; course < N; course++) {
            if (hasCycle(course, graph, state)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    private boolean hasCycle(
        int course,
        java.util.List<java.util.List<Integer>> graph,
        int[] state
    ) {
        if (state[course] == 1) {
            return true;
        }
        if (state[course] == 2) {
            return false;
        }

        state[course] = 1;
        for (int next : graph.get(course)) {
            if (hasCycle(next, graph, state)) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        state[course] = 2;
        return false;
    }
}
```

A course that depends on itself, such as `[2, 2]`, is caught immediately. With no prerequisites, every course is marked safe.

Time complexity: `O(N + E)`.
Space complexity: `O(N + E)`, including the graph and recursion stack. The input array is only read.

